WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.766
A broker holds messages in a queue until a consumer takes them.

2
00:00:06.766 --> 00:00:11.865
Five payment events for account 17 wait here, in the order they were published.

3
00:00:12.067 --> 00:00:16.237
The ledger service runs two copies, A and B, on this one queue.

4
00:00:16.237 --> 00:00:19.547
The broker gives each message to only one of them.

5
00:00:19.767 --> 00:00:22.795
Each may hold one unfinished message at a time.

6
00:00:22.795 --> 00:00:24.857
That cap is called the prefetch.

7
00:00:25.067 --> 00:00:30.407
The broker doesn't delete a message when it sends it, because the consumer might crash halfway.

8
00:00:30.407 --> 00:00:33.443
It waits for an acknowledgement that the work is done.

9
00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:35.319
m1 goes to A and m2 to B.

10
00:00:35.319 --> 00:00:40.644
Each books its payment and acknowledges it, and the broker deletes the message.

11
00:00:40.867 --> 00:00:46.333
Next, B takes m3, an authorization for 25, and A takes m4, its capture.

12
00:00:46.333 --> 00:00:51.184
Both are unacknowledged: sent, but still reserved in the queue.

13
00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:53.800
B crashes before it acknowledges m3.

14
00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:57.067
The broker puts m3 back at the head of the queue.

15
00:00:57.267 --> 00:00:59.207
But m4 has already left it.

16
00:00:59.207 --> 00:01:05.173
Consumer A books the capture of 25, for an authorization the ledger has never seen.

17
00:01:05.367 --> 00:01:09.621
Then A gets m3, marked as redelivered, and books it.

18
00:01:09.621 --> 00:01:10.521
m5 follows.

19
00:01:10.833 --> 00:01:14.000
Nothing was lost, and nothing was booked twice.

20
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.908
The queue kept its order, yet the ledger saw m4 before m3.

21
00:01:18.133 --> 00:01:24.071
A bigger prefetch makes this worse: more messages come back after a crash, and more get overtaken.

22
00:01:24.267 --> 00:01:29.116
One consumer per queue keeps the order, but then only one machine does the work.

23
00:01:29.333 --> 00:01:31.708
Other services need these payments too.

24
00:01:31.708 --> 00:01:38.712
The payment service sends each one to an exchange, which stores nothing and puts a copy in every queue bound to it.

25
00:01:38.933 --> 00:01:46.691
The ledger, the fraud check and the notification service each read their own queue, so every one of them sees every payment.

26
00:01:46.691 --> 00:01:47.629
That's fan-out.

27
00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:51.430
Fan-out between services, competing consumers within one.

28
00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:55.764
And a queue keeps its order only at the moment it hands a message out.
